      <section class="tg-section" id="summons">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">When the Summons Arrives</h2>

        <p>A jury summons is not an interruption to the Christian life. It is a place to live it out.
          For a few days or weeks, ordinary believers are asked to do something Scripture takes very
          seriously: weigh evidence, judge truthfully, and help a community do justice.</p>

        <p>Many Christians feel uneasy about it. Some wonder whether Jesus' words "do not judge" rule
          it out. Others dread the responsibility of deciding another person's future. Others simply
          see it as a civic chore to be escaped.</p>

        <p>The Bible speaks to all three. It does not mention modern juries, but it says a great deal
          about courts, witnesses, judges, and the God who loves justice. This study gathers that
          teaching and asks two questions: may a Christian serve, and if so, how should a Christian
          serve well?</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Translation</span>
          Scripture quotations on this page are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB).
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="may-serve">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">May a Christian Serve on a Jury?</h2>

        <p>Yes. Scripture treats civil justice as God's own work, done through human hands, and it
          calls believers to take part in the life of the community where God has placed them.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Government Is God's Servant for Justice</h3>

        <p>Paul writes that the governing authorities "have been appointed by God" and that the ruler
          "is God's servant for your good," an "agent of retribution to the wrongdoer"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 13:1-4">Romans 13:1–4</a>). Peter adds that governors are
          sent "to punish those who do wrong and to praise those who do right"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:13-14">1 Peter 2:13–14</a>). A jury is one of the means
          by which a modern state does exactly this. To serve on one is to share in a task God has
          assigned.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Seeking the Good of the City</h3>

        <p>To exiles in pagan Babylon, God said: "Seek the prosperity of the city to which I have sent
          you as exiles. Pray to the LORD on its behalf, for if it prospers, you too will prosper"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 29:7">Jeremiah 29:7</a>). Christians are not citizens of
          earth alone (<a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 3:20">Philippians 3:20</a>), but we are not
          absent from it either. Jesus taught us to "Give to Caesar what is Caesar's, and to God what
          is God's" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 22:21">Matthew 22:21</a>), and Paul urged us to
          give "respect to whom respect is due"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 13:7">Romans 13:7</a>). When the law asks citizens to serve,
          a willing response honors God.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Moses' Model: Capable People from Among the People</h3>

        <p>When Moses was overwhelmed with disputes, Jethro advised him to choose "capable men from
          among the people—God-fearing, trustworthy men who are averse to dishonest gain" and appoint
          them over the people (<a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 18:21">Exodus 18:21</a>; compare
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 1:13-17">Deuteronomy 1:13–17</a>). Justice in Israel
          was not left only to specialists. It was shared among ordinary, God-fearing members of the
          community. That is close to what a jury is.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What About "Do Not Judge"?</h3>

        <p>Jesus said, "Do not judge, or you will be judged"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 7:1">Matthew 7:1</a>). Read in context, He is condemning
          hypocritical, self-righteous condemnation: the person who ignores the beam in his own eye
          while picking at a speck in his brother's
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 7:3-5">Matthew 7:3–5</a>). The same Jesus said, "Stop
          judging by outward appearances, and start judging justly"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 7:24">John 7:24</a>). Paul expected believers to be able to
          settle disputes (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 6:1-5">1 Corinthians 6:1–5</a>).</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Key Principle</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">A juror is not asked to pronounce on anyone's soul or eternal
            standing. A juror is asked a narrower question: did the evidence prove what the law
            requires? Answering that honestly is not the judging Jesus forbids. It is the "judging
            justly" He commands.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Is It Ever Right to Avoid Service?</h3>

        <p>There are honest reasons to be excused: real hardship, a genuine conflict of interest, or a
          case you cannot judge impartially. Saying so plainly is right. What a Christian should not
          do is lie or exaggerate to escape service. "Simply let your 'Yes' be 'Yes,' and your 'No,'
          'No.'" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 5:37">Matthew 5:37</a>).</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="justice">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">God's Heart for Justice</h2>

        <p>The reason a Christian should take jury duty seriously is that God takes justice seriously.
          "Righteousness and justice are the foundation of Your throne"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 89:14">Psalm 89:14</a>). The Law's instructions to Israel's
          judges read almost like a juror's charge.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Pursue justice, not convenience</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">"Pursue justice, and justice alone."</div>
          <p>(<a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 16:20">Deuteronomy 16:20</a>) The Hebrew repeats the
            word for emphasis: <em>tsedeq, tsedeq</em>.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">No favoritism in either direction</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Neither the poor nor the rich may bend a verdict.</div>
          <p>"You must not show partiality to the poor or favoritism to the rich; you are to judge your
            neighbor fairly" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Leviticus 19:15">Leviticus 19:15</a>). Sympathy
            for the weak must not bend a verdict, and neither may respect for the powerful.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Do not follow the crowd</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Conviction is not a vote to be traded.</div>
          <p>"You shall not follow the crowd in wrongdoing. When you testify in a lawsuit, do not
            pervert justice by siding with the crowd" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 23:2">Exodus 23:2</a>).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Both errors are detestable</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Convicting the innocent and freeing the guilty are both wrong.</div>
          <p>"Acquitting the guilty and condemning the righteous— both are detestable to the LORD"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 17:15">Proverbs 17:15</a>).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Judgment belongs to God</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">A juror answers to Him.</div>
          <p>"Do not be intimidated by anyone, for judgment belongs to God"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 1:17">Deuteronomy 1:17</a>). King Jehoshaphat told
            his judges, "Consider carefully what you do, for you are not judging for man, but for the
            LORD" (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Chronicles 19:6-7">2 Chronicles 19:6–7</a>).</p>
        </div>

        <p>The prophets sum it up. "But let justice roll on like a river, and righteousness like an
          ever-flowing stream" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Amos 5:24">Amos 5:24</a>). "And what does the
          LORD require of you but to act justly, to love mercy, and to walk humbly with your God?"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Micah 6:8">Micah 6:8</a>). A juror gets a rare, concrete chance to
          do the first of these.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="conduct">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">How a Christian Juror Should Act</h2>

        <p>Faith does not give a juror secret knowledge about the case. It shapes the character a
          juror brings into the courtroom. Here is what that looks like at each stage.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">1. In Jury Selection: Tell the Whole Truth</h3>

        <p>During questioning (voir dire), answer honestly, even when the honest answer gets you
          dismissed or kept. Do not shade your views to get onto a jury, or off one. "Lying lips are
          detestable to the LORD, but those who deal faithfully are His delight"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 12:22">Proverbs 12:22</a>). If your convictions would
          keep you from following the court's instructions in a particular case, say so openly.</p>

        <p>If you are asked to take an oath, many Christians take it gladly; others, following
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 5:34-37">Matthew 5:34–37</a> and
          <a class="ref" data-ref="James 5:12">James 5:12</a>, ask to affirm instead. Courts allow
          either. What matters is that your word is true.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">2. During the Trial: Listen to the Whole Case</h3>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Withhold judgment until you have heard both sides.</strong> "The first to state
            his case seems right until another comes and cross-examines him"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 18:17">Proverbs 18:17</a>). "He who answers a matter
            before he hears it— this is folly and disgrace to him"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 18:13">Proverbs 18:13</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>Weigh the evidence, not your impressions.</strong> Scripture required more than
            one witness to establish a matter
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 19:15">Deuteronomy 19:15</a>) and demanded that
            charges be investigated "thoroughly"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 19:18">Deuteronomy 19:18</a>). Hold the prosecution
            to the standard of proof the law sets. That standard is a protection God's own law
            anticipated.</li>
          <li><strong>Remember that witnesses can be wrong or false.</strong> Jesus Himself was
            condemned on false testimony that was inconsistent
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 14:55-59">Mark 14:55–59</a>). Test what you hear.</li>
          <li><strong>Guard against partiality.</strong> Do not let a defendant's appearance, race,
            wealth, or manner sway you. James says, "do not show favoritism"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 2:1-4">James 2:1–4</a>). God is "showing no partiality and
            accepting no bribe" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 10:17">Deuteronomy 10:17</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>Follow the court's instructions.</strong> Do not research the case, visit the
            scene, or discuss it outside the jury room when told not to. Obeying these rules is part of
            submitting to authority for the Lord's sake
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:13">1 Peter 2:13</a>).</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">3. In Deliberation: Speak with Conviction and Humility</h3>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Pray for wisdom.</strong> "If any of you lacks wisdom, he should ask God"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 1:5">James 1:5</a>). You can pray silently before and
            during deliberation.</li>
          <li><strong>Be quick to listen, slow to speak</strong>
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 1:19">James 1:19</a>). Listen to fellow jurors, especially
            those who see it differently. They may have noticed what you missed.</li>
          <li><strong>Do not cave to pressure.</strong> If you are genuinely unconvinced, say so, even
            if you are the only one. <a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 23:2">Exodus 23:2</a> forbids
            siding with the crowd to pervert justice. A Christian's conscience is not for sale to end
            a long afternoon.</li>
          <li><strong>Do not be stubborn for pride's sake either.</strong> Be willing to change your
            mind when shown good reasons (<a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 12:15">Proverbs 12:15</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>Be gracious.</strong> Deliberation can get heated. "Let your speech always be
            gracious, seasoned with salt" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Colossians 4:6">Colossians 4:6</a>).
            Your conduct in that room is a witness to Christ, whether or not anyone knows you are a
            believer.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">4. Afterward: Rest in God</h3>

        <p>You did your part with the evidence you were given. You are not omniscient, and God does not
          expect you to be. Leave the outcome with the One who "will bring every deed into judgment,
          along with every hidden thing" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ecclesiastes 12:14">Ecclesiastes 12:14</a>).
          Respect the court's instructions on confidentiality.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="hard-questions">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Hard Questions</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What If the Law Itself Seems Unjust?</h3>

        <p>Christians obey the authorities, but not when obedience would mean sinning against God. "We
          must obey God rather than men" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 5:29">Acts 5:29</a>; compare
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Daniel 3:1-30">Daniel 3</a> and
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Daniel 6:1-28">6</a>). Most jury service never comes near that line.
          A juror is usually asked only to find facts honestly.</p>

        <p>If you know in advance that your conscience would not let you apply a particular law, the
          honest course is to say so during jury selection, not to conceal it and then quietly vote
          against the instructions. Truthfulness up front honors both God and the court.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What About Cases Involving the Death Penalty?</h3>

        <p>Scripture grants the state real authority to punish serious crime.
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 9:6">Genesis 9:6</a> grounds the penalty for murder in the
          image of God, and Paul says the ruler "does not carry the sword in vain"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 13:4">Romans 13:4</a>). Faithful Christians still differ on
          whether and how modern states should use capital punishment. Whatever your view, bring it
          honestly to jury selection, and if you serve, require the clearest proof. The Bible's own
          procedures for capital cases demanded multiple witnesses
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 17:6">Deuteronomy 17:6</a>) precisely because a life
          was at stake.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Isn't Mercy More Christlike Than a Guilty Verdict?</h3>

        <p>Mercy and justice are not enemies. They meet at the cross, where God demonstrates His
          righteousness "so as to be just and to justify the one who has faith in Jesus"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 3:26">Romans 3:26</a>). But a juror's role is to find what
          is true, not to grant pardon. The judge, the executive, and the victims hold other roles in
          which mercy may be shown. Personal vengeance is forbidden to us
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 12:19">Romans 12:19</a>), but Paul places the authority to
          punish with the state in the very next verses
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 13:4">Romans 13:4</a>). A truthful guilty verdict is not
          unloving. A false acquittal is not mercy; it is the injustice
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 17:15">Proverbs 17:15</a> condemns.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What If the Case Is Disturbing or the Burden Feels Too Heavy?</h3>

        <p>Some trials involve violence, abuse, or grief that stays with jurors long after. That weight
          is real. Bring it to God: "Cast your burden upon the LORD and He will sustain you"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 55:22">Psalm 55:22</a>). Ask the court about juror support
          resources if they exist. Once the trial ends, talk with your pastor or a trusted believer,
          within whatever limits the court has set.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="prayer">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">A Juror's Prayer</h2>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Lord, You are the Judge of all the earth, and You always do what is right. Give me ears to
          hear the whole case and a heart that shows no partiality. Keep me from rushing to judgment
          and from bending to pressure. Let me love truth more than comfort, and justice more than
          ease. Give wisdom to the judge, honesty to the witnesses, and grace to all of us in the jury
          room. Comfort the victims, have mercy on the accused, and draw everyone in this courtroom to
          Yourself. And when my part is done, let me rest in Your perfect justice. In Jesus' name,
          amen.
        </blockquote>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Serving Under the Righteous Judge</h3>

        <p>Every human court is imperfect. Witnesses forget, evidence is incomplete, and jurors see
          only part of the picture. Christians can serve without fear because no verdict of ours is
          final. "Will not the Judge of all the earth do what is right?"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 18:25">Genesis 18:25</a>). God "has set a day when He will
          judge the world with justice by the Man He has appointed"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 17:31">Acts 17:31</a>).</p>

        <p>That Judge is also our Savior. He stood in a courtroom Himself, falsely accused and unjustly
          condemned, so that guilty people like us could be declared righteous. A Christian juror
          serves in His shadow: honest, careful, humble, and unafraid, doing justice for a season
          because the Lord loves it, and waiting for the day when He will set every wrong right.</p>
      </section>

